1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,027
Here's a normal DeFi request:

2
00:00:02,067 --> 00:00:06,509
stake ETH with Lido and borrow USDC against it on Aave.

3
00:00:06,549 --> 00:00:09,766
Same thing on Solana, with Marinade and Kamino.

4
00:00:11,816 --> 00:00:14,597
On the left, Claude Code with no harness.

5
00:00:14,637 --> 00:00:18,347
It hunts for contract addresses, asks you for an RPC,

6
00:00:18,387 --> 00:00:21,877
hits a revert because Aave takes wstETH,

7
00:00:21,917 --> 00:00:26,196
not stETH, and then wants your private key in a .env file.

8
00:00:26,236 --> 00:00:29,621
On the right, the same model through Aomi reads the state,

9
00:00:29,661 --> 00:00:32,971
simulates every step, and ships one bundle.

10
00:00:33,122 --> 00:00:35,091
The point of a harness is performance,

11
00:00:35,131 --> 00:00:37,529
token efficiency, and security.

12
00:00:37,569 --> 00:00:40,245
Frontier models can kind of transact without one,

13
00:00:40,285 --> 00:00:42,184
but a harness makes them work better,

14
00:00:42,224 --> 00:00:45,191
so if you want to productionize agentic finance,

15
00:00:45,231 --> 00:00:46,475
it's a no-brainer.

16
00:00:47,025 --> 00:00:49,620
Reading chain and wallet state is deterministic,

17
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:52,836
so Aomi shrinks it to a software function:

18
00:00:52,876 --> 00:00:55,316
one call instead of a dozen.

19
00:00:57,166 --> 00:00:59,297
Every call runs on a fork first.

20
00:00:59,337 --> 00:01:02,223
A revert comes back to the model as back pressure,

21
00:01:02,263 --> 00:01:04,533
like failing tests for a coding agent,

22
00:01:04,573 --> 00:01:06,804
and only a green run gets committed.

23
00:01:07,354 --> 00:01:08,649
Once everything passes,

24
00:01:08,689 --> 00:01:11,993
Aomi compresses the sequence into one transaction:

25
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:14,268
a smart wallet bundle on Ethereum,

26
00:01:14,308 --> 00:01:16,939
one versioned transaction on Solana.

27
00:01:16,979 --> 00:01:19,244
That saves gas and settlement time,

28
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:21,404
and the model does less.

29
00:01:21,954 --> 00:01:24,190
And the key never goes to the model.

30
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:27,289
Commit hands your wallet a signable transaction,

31
00:01:27,329 --> 00:01:28,323
and you sign it,

32
00:01:28,363 --> 00:01:31,318
or a scoped session or an agent wallet

33
00:01:31,358 --> 00:01:33,292
signs within the limits you set.

34
00:01:33,332 --> 00:01:38,047
On Solana, a Swig role enforces those limits onchain.

35
00:01:38,797 --> 00:01:41,933
Performance, token efficiency, and security.

36
00:01:44,483 --> 00:01:46,277
The model interprets the intent,

37
00:01:46,317 --> 00:01:47,682
your wallet holds the key,

38
00:01:47,722 --> 00:01:51,056
and Aomi facilitates everything in between.
